course: dictionary
lesson: 13645
title: Infallible: never wrong
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Infallible is a long word, but you can break it down. The small part in- means "not", and fallible means able to make mistakes. So infallible means never wrong, never making a mistake. Context helps too: «Nobody is infallible, not even the head teacher.» The words nobody and not even tell you that everyone makes mistakes sometimes.
explain: The word can also describe something that always works. «Mum's infallible cure for hiccups» means her cure always works. To check, swap in the meaning: "Mum's never-wrong cure for hiccups" still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Scientists use the same idea in a careful way. «No test is infallible, so we check our results.» Because no test is perfect, we check results before we trust them. Infallibly is the adverb: «She infallibly remembers every birthday.» It means she never forgets, without any mistakes.
fact: Infallible means never wrong, never making a mistake.
fact: Infallible can also mean always working or always succeeding, as in «an infallible method».
fact: The adverb is infallibly, and the noun is infallibility.
fact: In church teaching, Catholics believe the Pope is infallible when he solemnly teaches on faith and morals; this is called papal infallibility.
fact: In science, no test or person is infallible, so we check results.
exercise: «Nobody is infallible, not even the head teacher.» What does infallible mean? | *Never wrong | Always late | Very tired | Loud and cheerful
exercise: «Mum's infallible cure for hiccups» works every time. What does infallible suggest here? | *Always works | Tastes horrible | Makes you sneeze | Sometimes works, if you are lucky
exercise: Which part of infallible means "not"? | *in- | -ible | -able | fal
test: «No test is infallible, so we check our results.» What does this sentence tell you? | Tests are only for teachers | Tests are always easy | *No test is perfect, so checking matters | Results never need checking
test: Which word is the adverb form of infallible? | fallen | *infallibly | falsely | infallibility
exam: «She infallibly remembers every birthday in the class.» What does infallibly mean here? | Loudly and proudly | *Always correctly, without a mistake | Sometimes, if she feels like it | Forgetfully
